{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-80f05c03f534710d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "EXPLICIT AND STABLE PSEUDOSPECTRAL TIME-DOMAIN\nMETHOD FOR THE FÖPPL–VON KÁRMÁN EQUATIONS\nVictor Zheleznov1 , Stefan Bilbao2\n2\n\n1\nAcoustics and Audio Group, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK\nSTMS (UMR9912), IRCAM, CNRS, Sorbonne Université, Paris, France\n\nThis contribution has been accepted based on the submitted abstract. The submitted manuscript was not reviewed and is reproduced\nhere without edits or corrections by the conference board. Neither the EAA nor the AAA take responsibility for its contents.\n\narXiv:2608.06139v1 [cs.SD] 6 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nModal synthesis is a widely-used technique for simulation of musical instrument dynamics. In the linear\ncase, a modal decomposition leads to an uncoupled system of damped and forced harmonic oscillators which\ncan be efficiently solved by standard time-stepping\nmethods. However, extensions to nonlinear problems\nare challenging due to the presence of products of\nmodal expansions in the governing equations. In the\ncase of the Föppl–von Kármán plate, the nonlinear\ncoupling between the modes is described by a fourthorder tensor and is prohibitively expensive to evaluate\nin the modal domain. In this work, we propose a\npseudospectral method in which the products are evaluated on a grid in the spatial domain while spatial\nderivatives are computed exactly in the modal domain.\nDiscrete sine and cosine transforms between the modal\nand spatial domains are used to impose simply supported boundary conditions for the plate. Finally, we\nprove non-negativity of the nonlinear potential energy\nof the system and employ a scalar auxiliary variable\ntechnique for explicit and stable time integration in\nthe modal domain. As a result, we reduce the computational cost of modal synthesis while preserving its\nadvantages like a precise control over the simulated\nfrequency range. Sound examples are presented.\nKeywords: nonlinear plate vibration, pseudospectral\nmethod, modal synthesis.\n\n1. Introduction\nHigh-amplitude vibration of a thin plate is perhaps\nthe most dramatic example of nonlinear behaviour\nin musical acoustics, leading to characteristic pitch\nglides and crashes in percussion instruments. The\nFöppl–von Kármán equations are commonly used to\nCorresponding author: v.zheleznov@ed.ac.uk.\nCopyright: ©2026 Victor Zheleznov et al. This is an openaccess article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium,\nprovided the original author and source are credited.\n\nreproduce these perceptually-important effects in audio rate simulations [1]. One of the techniques to\nnumerically solve such equations is known as modal\nsynthesis [2]. This approach falls into a category of\nGalerkin spectral methods where the governing equations are made orthogonal to basis functions of a finite\nseries that approximate the solution [3]. In the case\nof the Föppl–von Kármán equations, this leads to a\ncomputationally-expensive model [2] which requires a\nGPU for real-time applications [4].\nIn this work, we employ a pseudospectral method\nwhere the governing equations are satisfied for a grid\nof collocation points in the spatial domain [3]. Such\nmethods are well-suited for nonlinear problems since\nthey give rise to vector products rather than functional series that require determination of expansion\ncoefficients [5]. We decompose unknown functions\nusing Fourier sine and cosine series to satisfy simply supported boundary conditions for a rectangular\nplate [6]. For time integration, we rewrite the governing equations in the modal domain to ensure exact\ndiscretisation of the linear part of the problem [1]\nand employ a scalar auxiliary variable technique [7].\nThis results in an explicit and stable numerical solver\nfor the Föppl–von Kármán equations in contrast to\nan implicit and iterative scheme previously used by\nKirby and Yosibash [8]. Compared to a modal approach developed by Russo et al. [9] in the related\ncontext of nonlinear string vibration, spatial derivatives are computed exactly in the modal domain rather\nthan approximated by finite differences in the spatial\ndomain.\nThe paper is organised as follows. The continuous\nmodel of a thin plate is given in Section 2. In Section 3\nmodal equations using the pseudospectral method are\nderived, and then discretised in time using an explicit\nand stable numerical solver in Section 4. Numerical\nresults are presented in Section 5. Sound examples\nare available on the accompanying page1 .\n1 https://victorzheleznov.github.io/fa2026\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\f2. Continuous Model\nThe Föppl–von Kármán equations in a scaled form\nover a unit area region D are given as follows [1]:\n∂t2 u = − κ 2 ∆2 u − 2σ0 ∂t u + 2σ1 ∂t ∆u +\n+ κ 2 L(u, φ) + δ(r − re )fe (t),\n∆2 φ = − L(u, u).\n\n(1a)\n(1b)\n\nHere u = u(r, t) is the transverse displacement and\nφ = φ(r, t) is the Airy function. Both are defined\nfor spatial coordinates r = (x, y) ∈ D ⊂ R2 and time\nt ∈ R+ . Partial derivatives with respect to x, y and\nt are denoted as ∂x , ∂y and ∂t , respectively. The\nLaplacian operator is ∆ = ∂x2 + ∂y2 and the biharmonic\noperator is ∆2 . The nonlinearity is introduced by the\noperator L defined for functions α(r) and β(r) as:\nL(α, β) = ∂x2 α∂y2 β + ∂y2 α∂x2 β − 2∂x ∂y α∂x ∂y β.\n\nwhere famp is the excitation amplitude in sec−2 and\nTe is the excitation duration in sec.\nWe can obtain an energy balance for (1a) by taking\nan L2 (D) inner product with ∂t u:\ndH\n= Pe − Pd + Bd + Blin + Bnl + B′nl .\ndt\n\n(2)\n\nwhere ∥·∥D is the L2 (D) norm.\nThe terms Pe and Pd describe input and dissipated\npower, respectively, and are of the following form:\nPe = ∂t u(re , t)fe (t),\n2\n\n[∂n ∂τ u(φ∂τ ∂t u − ∂t u∂τ φ) +\n∂D\n\n(3d)\n\n+ (∆u − ∂n2 u)(∂t u∂n φ − φ∂n ∂t u)] ds,\nT\nwhere ∂n ≜ ∇T\nr n and ∂τ ≜ ∇r τ are normal and\ntangential derivatives, respectively.\n√\nWe consider a rectangular region D = [0, η]×[0, 1/√η]\nwith an aspect ratio η > 0 and employ the simply\nsupported boundary conditions [12]:\n\nu = ∆u = 0,\n\n∂n φ = ∂n ∆φ = 0,\n\nr ∈ ∂D. (4)\n\nAs can be seen, the boundary terms (3) vanish under\nthe boundary conditions (4). Note that the energy H\ntakes the form (2) only under a fixed termination for\nthe displacement u [1]. Initial conditions are assumed\nto be zero, unless stated otherwise.\n\n3.1. Modal Decomposition\nThe boundary conditions (4) allow a closed-form solution for modal shapes [13]. We decompose the displacement u and the Airy function φ using orthonormal\nsine and cosine series with modal coefficients qij (t)\nand ξij (t), respectively:\nu=\n\nMx My\nX\nX\n\n2 sin(kx,i x) sin(ky,j y)qij (t),\n\n(5a)\n\n2αi αj cos(kx,i x) cos(ky,j y)ξij (t),\n\n(5b)\n\ni=1 j=1\n\nφ=\n\nMx My\nX\nX\ni=0 j=0\n\nwhere Mx and My are the number of modes for each\nspatial direction. A scaling factor αi is defined as\n√\nα0 = 1/ 2, αi = 1, i ̸= 0. Spatial components kx,i and\nky,j of a wavevector and a wavenumber kij are:\n\nThe energy H is defined as:\n1\nκ2\nκ2\n2\n2\n2\n∥∂t u∥D +\n∥∆u∥D +\n∥∆φ∥D ,\n2\n2\n4\n\nI\n\n3. Pseudospectral Method\n\nSystem (1) is characterised by a stiffness parameter\nκ and frequency-dependent loss parameters σ0 ≥ 0\nand σ1 ≥ 0, all with dimensions of sec−1 . It is excited\nat the location re = (xe , ye ) by the function fe (t)\ncorresponding to a strike [10]:\n(\n\u0002\n\u0001\u0003\n1\nfamp 1 − cos 2πt\n,\nt ∈ [0, Te ]\n2\nT\ne\nfe (t) =\n0,\notherwise\n\nH=\n\nB′nl = κ 2\n\n2\n\nPd = 2σ0 ∥∂t u∥D + 2σ1 ∥∂t ∇r u∥D ,\nwhere ∇r is the gradient operator with respect to r.\nFor a point on the boundary ∂D with normal and\ntangent unit vectors n and τ , respectively, we can\nwrite the boundary terms as [11]:\nI\nBd = 2σ1\n∂t u∂n ∂t u ds,\n(3a)\nI ∂D\nBlin = κ 2\n(∆u∂n ∂t u − ∂t u∂n ∆u) ds,\n(3b)\n∂D\nI\nκ2\nBnl =\n(∆∂t φ∂n φ − φ∂n ∆∂t φ) ds,\n(3c)\n2 ∂D\n\niπ\nkx,i = √ ,\nη\n\n√\nky,j = jπ η,\n\nkij =\n\nq\n\n2 + k2 .\nkx,i\ny,j\n\nCompared to the displacement u, the Airy function\nφ includes one-dimensional modes when either i = 0\nor j = 0 and a constant mode when both i = 0 and\nj = 0. Without loss of generality, we zero out the\nconstant mode ξ00 ≜ 0 since the Airy function φ is\ndefined up to an arbitrary bilinear function of spatial\ncoordinates [14].\n3.2. Spatial Discretisation\nWe consider a staggered spatial grid defined as:\nxi =\n\n√ i + 12\nη\n,\nNx\n\n1 j + 12\nyj = √\n,\nη Ny\n\ni=0,...,Nx −1\nj=0,...,Ny −1,\n\n(6)\n\nwhere Nx and Ny are the number of collocation points\nfor each spatial direction. We define grid functions\nuij (t) and φij (t) that represent decompositions (5)\nevaluated on the grid (6). We can rewrite them in a\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\fvectorised form as:\np\nu = Nx Ny (Sx ⊗ Sy )T q,\np\nφ = Nx Ny (Cx ⊗ Cy )T ξ.\n\nf2 (q, q) = p\n\nHere vectors u ∈ RNx Ny , φ ∈ RNx Ny , q ∈ RMx My and\nξ ∈ R(Mx +1)(My +1) are formed through row-major\nvectorisation of corresponding grid functions uij (t)\nand φij (t) and modal coefficients qij (t) and ξij (t).\nMatrices Sa and Ca for a ∈ {x, y} are truncated\nmatrices of the orthonormal type-II discrete sine and\ncosine transforms, respectively, defined as [15]:\nr\n\u0014\n\u0015\ni(j + 12 )π\n2\ni=1,...,Ma\n[Sa ]ij =\nsin\n,\nj=0,...,Na −1,\nNa\nNa\nr\n\u0014\n\u0015\ni(j + 12 )π\n2\ni=0,...,Ma\n[Ca ]ij =\nαi cos\n, j=0,...,N\n.\na −1\nNa\nNa\nAssuming Na > Ma , rows of these matrices are orT\nthonormal vectors so that Sa ST\na = IMa and Ca Ca =\nIMa +1 where IN denotes an N × N identity matrix.\nThe Kronecker product ⊗ is used to construct twodimensional transforms.\n3.3. Modal Equations\nWe substitute decompositions (5) into system (1) and\nrequire that it is satisfied on the grid (6). Leftmultiplying\n(1a) and (1b) p\nafter discretisation by\np\n(1/ Nx Ny )(Sx ⊗ Sy ) and (1/ Nx Ny )(Cx ⊗ Cy ), respectively, we obtain:\nq̈ + 2σ ⊙ q̇ + ω 2 ⊙ q = κ 2 f1 (q, ξ) + je fe (t),\n4\n\nk ⊙ ξ = −f2 (q, q).\n\n(7a)\n(7b)\n\n(Mx +1)(My +1)\n\nHere a vector k ∈ R\nis a row-major vectorisation of wavenumbers kij , i = 0, . . . , Mx , j =\n0, . . . , My . In addition, we define k̂ ∈ RMx My by\nexcluding indices i = 0 and j = 0 before vectorisation. Modal frequencies ω ∈ RMx My and damping\ncoefficients σ ∈ RMx My are obtained as:\nω = κ k̂2 ,\n\nσ = σ0 + σ1 k̂2 .\n\nNote that damping coefficients σ can be generalised\nto represent an arbitrary loss profile. The Hadamard\n(elementwise) product ⊙ is used to multiply vectors\nand the power operator indicates elementwise raising.\nThe function fe (t) in (7a) is weighted by modal shapes\n2 sin(kx,i xe ) sin(ky,j ye ) at the location re , consolidated\ninto a vector je ∈ RMx My . This vector represents\ncoefficients of an orthonormal sine series for the delta\nfunction δ(r − re ).\n3.4. Nonlinearity\nSystem (7) is in the same form as in a modal approach [2]. The difference lies in the definition of the\nnonlinear functions f1 (q, ξ) and f2 (q, q):\nf1 (q, ξ) = p\n\n1\n(Sx ⊗ Sy )l1 (u, φ),\nNx Ny\n\n(8a)\n\n1\n(Cx ⊗ Cy )l2 (u, u),\nNx Ny\n\n(8b)\n\nwhere discretised operators l1 (u, φ) and l2 (u, u) give\nexact values of the operator L on the grid (6). To\ndefine these discretised operators, we require differentiation matrices for grid functions u and φ that exactly\nevaluate derivatives of the displacement u and the Airy\nfunction φ on the grid (6), given decompositions (5).\nFirst, we obtain differentiation matrices Dxx,s and\nDxx,c for derivatives ∂x2 u and ∂x2 φ, respectively:\n\u0001\n2\nDxx,s = − ST\nx K̂x Sx ⊗ INy ,\n\u0001\n2\nDxx,c = − CT\nx Kx Cx ⊗ INy ,\nwhere Kx = diag(kx,0 , . . . , kx,Mx ) and K̂x is the matrix Kx without the wavenumber kx,0 . Note that we\ncan split Dxx,s = Dx,ĉ Dx,s into first-order differentiation matrices defined as:\n\u0001\nDx,s = ĈT\nDx,ĉ = −DT\nx K̂x Sx ⊗ INy ,\nx,s ,\nwhere Ĉx is the matrix Cx without the first row. This\nis not be possible for Dxx,c since it accounts for onedimensional modes and a constant mode which are\nnot present in a discrete sine transform. By analogy,\nwe can construct differentiation matrices Dyy,s , Dyy,c ,\nDy,s and Dy,ĉ in the y-direction.\nSecond, we obtain differentiation matrices Dxy,s and\nDxy,ĉ for derivatives ∂x ∂y u and ∂x ∂y φ, respectively:\nDxy,s = Dx,s Dy,s ,\n\nDxy,ĉ = DT\nxy,s ,\n\nwhere Dxy,ĉ φ is exact since one-dimensional modes\nand a constant mode do not affect the value of ∂x ∂y φ.\nFinally, we can define l1 (u, φ) and l2 (u, u) as follows:\nl1 (u, φ) = Dxx,s u ⊙ Dyy,c φ + Dyy,s u ⊙ Dxx,c φ −\n− 2Dxy,s u ⊙ Dxy,ĉ φ,\nl2 (u, u) = Dxx,s u ⊙ Dyy,s u + Dyy,s u ⊙ Dxx,s u −\n− 2Dxy,s u ⊙ Dxy,s u.\nTo mitigate aliasing errors due to products in the\nspatial domain, we employ the 3/2 rule [3] by setting\nNa = ⌈3Ma/2⌉ + 1, a ∈ {x, y}. Under this choice\naliasing would occur only for the highest third of\nwavenumbers which are filtered out by discrete sine\nand cosine transforms in (8). For the next section, it\nis useful to define “brick-wall” filters in the spatial\ndomain as symmetric matrices Hs ≜ (Sx ⊗ Sy )T (Sx ⊗\nSy ) and Hc ≜ (Cx ⊗ Cy )T (Cx ⊗ Cy ).\nThe computational cost of nonlinearity is mainly determined by discrete sine and cosine transforms. For\nsimplicity, assume a square plate so that number of\nmodes and collocation points is the same in both spatial directions. Applying transforms independently\nin each dimension using naive matrix multiplication,\nit takes O(Nx3 ) operations to evaluate f1 (q, ξ). This\nestimate can be reduced to O(Nx2 log2 Nx ) if fast trans-\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\fforms are employed [15]. In contrast, it can be shown\nthat a modal approach [2] would require evaluation of\nO(Mx4 ) = O(Nx4 ) coupling terms with products qij ξlm .\nThis indicates that the pseudospectral method is significantly more efficient for a large number of modes.\n3.5. Nonlinear Potential Energy\nIn view of using a scalar auxiliary variable technique,\nwe require a non-negative potential V (q) so that:\nf1 (q, ξ) = −∇q V (q).\nTo derive this potential, we need to specify product\nrules for differentiation matrices.\nAssume grid functions α and β that are bandlimited\nwith respect to a transform (Sx ⊗ Sy ) and a grid function γ that is bandlimited with respect to a transform\n(Ĉx ⊗ Ĉy ). Considering product-to-sum identities for\nsine and cosine functions, the following product rules\nhold:\nDxx,c Hc (α ⊙ β) = Hc (Dxx,s α ⊙ β + α ⊙ Dxx,s β +\n+ 2Dx,s α ⊙ Dx,s β),\nDxy,s Hs (α ⊙ γ) = Hc (Dxy,s α ⊙ γ + α ⊙ Dxy,ĉ γ +\n+ Dx,s α ⊙ Dy,ĉ γ + Dy,s α ⊙ Dx,ĉ γ).\nBy analogy, we can define a product rule for Dyy,c .\nTaking a dot product of f1 (q, ξ) with q̇, exploiting the\nfact that “brick-wall” filters do not change bandlimited\ngrid functions and using the product rules, we obtain:\nq̇T f1 (q, ξ) =\n\n1\nu̇T l1 (u, φ) =\nNx Ny\n\n1\n(Hc Dyy,c φ)T (Dxx,s u ⊙ u̇) +\nNx Ny\n1\n+\n(Hc Dxx,c φ)T (Dyy,s u ⊙ u̇) −\nNx Ny\n2\n−\n(Hs Dxy,ĉ φ)T (Dxy,s u ⊙ u̇) =\nNx Ny\n1\n=\nφT Hc l2 (u, u̇) = ξ T f2 (q, q̇),\nNx Ny\n\n=\n\nIt can be shown that f2 (q, q) has a zero constant mode\nso that (7b) is fully satisfied.\n\n4. Numerical Solver\n4.1. Quadratisation\np\nWe introduce an auxiliary variable ψ ≜ 2V (q) + ε\nfor an arbitrary constant ε > 0 and rewrite (7a) as:\nq̈ + 2σ ⊙ q̇ + ω 2 ⊙ q = −κ 2 ψg + je fe (t),\nT\n\nψ̇ = g q̇,\n\n(9a)\n(9b)\n\nwhere g ≜ gstd (q) = ∇q ψ. Furthermore, we add a\ncontrol term to (9) as described by Risse et al. [7]\nto reduce\nthe drift between numerical values of ψ\np\nand 2V (q) + ε in discrete time. This results in\nmodification of the coupling term g:\ng ≜ gstd (q) + gmod (q, q̇, ψ),\n\u0010\n\u0011 sign(q̇)\np\n,\ngmod (q, q̇, ψ) = −λ0 ψ − 2V (q) + ε\n∥q̇∥1\nwhere λ0 ≥ 0 is the control parameter in sec−1 .\n4.2. Time Discretisation\nWe choose a time step T in sec, yelding a sampling\nrate fs = 1/T . Then, we approximate q(t) and ψ(t)\n1\nby time series qn and ψ n− /2 on interleaved temporal\nn\nn−1/2\ngrids t = nT and t\n= (n − 1/2)T for n ∈ N. We\nintroduce one-step difference and averaging operators\nδt± and µt± , acting on time series as:\nδt± qn = ±\n\nqn±1 − qn\n,\nT\n\nµt± qn =\n\nqn±1 + qn\n.\n2\n\nUsing centred approximations δt· = δt+ µt− and\nδtt = δt+ δt− for first-order and second-order time\nderivatives, respectively, we define the scheme as:\nδtt qn + 2σ̃ ⊙ δt· qn + ω̃ 2 ⊙ qn =\n1\n= −κ 2 µt+ ψ n− /2 gn + je fen ,\n1\n\nδt+ ψ n− /2 = (gn )T δt· qn ,\n\n(10a)\n(10b)\n1\n\nwhich represents a discretised “triple self-adjointness”\nproperty of the operator L [14]. Using the symmetry\nproperty f2 (q, q̇) = f2 (q̇, q), we obtain:\nξ T f2 (q, q̇) = ξ T\n\n1 d\nf2 (q, q) =\n2 dt\n(7b) T 1 d\n= ξ\n(−k4 ⊙ ξ) = −V̇ (q).\n2 dt\n\nThe potential V (q) is defined as:\nV (q) =\n\n1 2\n2\nk ⊙ ξ(q) 2 ≥ 0,\n4\n\nwhere ∥·∥p for p ≥ 1 is the p-norm. To evaluate V (q),\nwe substitute the solution for ξ = ξ(q) from (7b),\nwhere the biharmonic operator can be easily inverted\nby elementwise division for all but the constant mode.\n\nwhere gn = gstd (qn ) + gmod (µt− qn , δt− qn , ψ n− /2 )\nand fen = fe (tn ). In addition, modal frequencies\nand damping coefficients are adjusted to mitigate\nnumerical dispersion and ensure exact discretisation\nof the linear part of (9a) [1]:\n\u0010 √\n\u0011\n−σij T\n2\n2\n−2σij T\n1\n−\n2e\ncos\nT\nω\n−\nσ\nij\nij + e\n2\n2\nω̃ij = 2\n,\nT\n1 + e−2σij T\n1 1 − e−2σij T\nσ̃ij =\n.\nT 1 + e−2σij T\nWe can obtain an energy balance for (10a) by taking a dot product with δt· qn and employing product\nidentities for discrete-time operators [1]:\n1\n\nδt+ H n− /2 = (δt· qn )T je fen − (δt· qn )T (2σ̃ ⊙ δt· qn ),\n|\n{z\n} |\n{z\n}\n≜Pen\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n≜Pdn\n\n\f×10−13\n\n×10−15\n1\n\n10−1\n0\n\n0\n\n−1\n\n−1\n\nDrift\n\nEnergy error\n\n1\n\nλ0 = 103\n\nλ0 = 0\n\n101\n\n10−3\n10−5\n\n0\n\n50\nTime [ms]\n\n100\n\n0\n\n50\nTime [ms]\n\n100\n\nFigure 1: Relative energy error E n (left) and its enlarged section that reveals numerical quantisation (right).\nHorizontal lines indicate multiples of machine accuracy\n2−52 ≈ 2.2 × 10−16 .\n1\n\nwhere the energy H n− /2 is defined as:\n1\n\nH n− /2 =\n\n1\n1\n2\n∥δt− qn ∥2 + (qn )T (ω̃ 2 ⊙ qn−1 ) +\n2\n2\nκ 2 n−1/2 \u00012\n+\nψ\n.\n2\nn−1/2\n\nThe second term in H\ncan be bounded to guarantee non-negativity of numerical energy [16], resulting\nin a stability condition ωij < πfs , i.e, frequencies\nup to a Nyquist limit can be reproduced. Given the\ndefinition of ωij , the number of modes in simulation\nwould then be smaller than the product Mx My .\nTo initialise the scheme, we require values q0\n1/2\n1\nand\n=\np q and set the auxiliary variable as ψ\n1\n2V (µt− q ) + ε. For time stepping, (10) can be written in an explicit update form after a matrix inversion\nusing the Sherman-Morrison formula [9].\n\n5. Numerical Results\nThe source code used for numerical tests can be found\nin the accompanying repository2 . All simulations\nwere done at fs = 44.1 kHz. A rectangular plate\nwas used with a ratio η = 1.1. Frequency-dependent\nloss parameters were set as σ0 = 1.3 and σ1 = 10−4 ,\nresulting in a T60 decay time of around 10 sec for\nlower frequencies. The simulated frequency range was\nreduced to 17 kHz away from the Nyquist limit to\navoid anomalous behaviour of the scheme [1].\nAs a first case study, a large plate with κ = 8 was used,\nresulting in 1027 modes. To check energy conservation,\nthe plate was initialised in its first mode of vibration\nas q00 = 5 without additional excitation, i.e., Pen ≡ 0.\nStep-by-step relative energy error E n is defined as:\nn+1/2\n\nEn =\n\nHtot\n\nn−1/2\n\n− Htot\n\nn−1/2\n⌊Htot ⌋2\n\nn−1/2\n\n, Htot\n\n1\n\n= H n− /2 +\n\nn−1\nX\n\nT Pdi ,\n\ni=1\n\nwhere ⌊·⌋2 denotes the nearest power of two rounded\ntowards zero. As demonstrated on Fig. 1, the energy\nerror remains constant to machine accuracy during\n2 https://github.com/victorzheleznov/fa2026\n\n10−7\n\n0\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n5\nTime [sec]\n\n6\n\n7\n\n8\n\n9\n\nFigure 2: Relative drift Dn− /2 . Vertical dashed lines\nindicate the moments in time when the plate is excited.\n1\n\nthe simulation.\nTo test drift regulation, the plate was repeatedly excited using the function fe (t) with famp = 2 × 106\nand Te = 2 ms at the location re = (0.17, 0.42). The\n1\nrelative drift Dn− /2 is defined as:\np\n1\nψ n− /2 − 2V (µt− qn ) + ε\nn−1/2\np\n,\nD\n=\nmax 2V (µt− qn ) + ε\nn\n\nwhere the maximum value is taken over the whole\nduration of simulation. Comparison between the cases\nwith and without drift regulation is given in Fig. 2.\nAs can be seen, the maximum value of the relative\ndrift is reduced by two orders of magnitude and the\ndrift steadily decreases towards zero by the control\nterm with λ0 = 103 up to the next excitation. In\ncontrast, the auxiliary variable remains incorrect over\nthe whole duration of simulation in the case λ0 = 0.\nAs a second case study, a small plate with κ = 60\nwas considered, resulting in 127 modes. Fig. 3 provides spectrograms for displacement of the plate at\nincreasing excitation amplitudes, taken at the location ro = (0.64, 0.79). Under a low amplitude, linear\nbehaviour is recovered with distinct modal frequencies. At a higher amplitude, pitch glide effects appear.\nThen, a wideband noise is observed, resulting in a\ncrash-like sound. As demonstrated on the rightmost\nspectrogram in Fig. 3, instantaneous frequencies are\nsignificantly affected by the removal of the control\nterm, leading to audible artefacts. Readers are encouraged to listen to sound examples presented on the\naccompanying page3 .\n\n6. Conclusion\nAn explicit and stable pseudospectral method for simulation of the Föppl–von Kármán equations has been\npresented here. Numerical tests at an audio rate have\nconfirmed energy conservation of the scheme and drift\nregulation properties of the scalar auxiliary variable\ntechnique, which were previously reported only for\nthe case of nonlinear string vibration [7]. Additional\nwork is required to evaluate real-time capability of the\nproposed method in comparison to a finite difference\n3 https://victorzheleznov.github.io/fa2026\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\ffamp = 2 × 105 , λ0 = 103\n\nFrequency [kHz]\n\n4\n\nfamp = 2 × 106 , λ0 = 103\n\nfamp = 2 × 107 , λ0 = 103\n\nfamp = 2 × 107 , λ0 = 0\n\n3\n2\n1\n0\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\nFigure 3: Spectrograms for displacement of the plate at increasing excitation amplitudes, as indicated. The third and\nfourth spectrograms are given at identical excitation amplitudes to demonstrate drift regulation of the numerical solver.\n\napproach [10] using an optimised C++ implementation.\nIn addition, extension to other boundary conditions is\nneeded to further evaluate suitability of pseudospectral methods to problems in musical acoustics.\n\n[8]\n\n7. Acknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the SGSAH AHRC\nDoctoral Training Partnership [grant number\nAH/R012717/1]; and the University of Edinburgh.\nThe authors would like to thank Olivier Thomas for\ndiscussions on boundary conditions for the Föppl–von\nKármán equations.\n\n[9]\n\nReferences\n[1] S. Bilbao, Numerical Sound Synthesis: Finite\nDifference Schemes and Simulation in Musical\nAcoustics. Chichester, UK: John Wiley & Sons,\n2009.\n\n[10]\n\n[2] M. Ducceschi and C. Touzé, “Modal approach\nfor nonlinear vibrations of damped impacted\nplates: Application to sound synthesis of gongs\nand cymbals,” J. Sound Vib., vol. 344, pp. 313–\n331, 2015.\n\n[11]\n\n[12]\n\n[3] J. P. Boyd, Chebyshev and Fourier Spectral\nMethods. New York: Dover Publications, 2000.\n[4] R. Diaz and M. Sandler, “Fast Differentiable\nModal Simulation of Non-Linear Strings, Membranes, and Plates,” in Proc. 28th Int. Conf. Digital Audio Effects, Ancona, Italy, 2025, pp. 134–\n141.\n[5] B. Fornberg, A Practical Guide to Pseudospectral Methods. Cambridge University Press, 1996.\n[6] E. S. Wise, J. Jaros, B. T. Cox, and B. E. Treeby,\n“Pseudospectral Time-Domain (PSTD) Methods for the Wave Equation: Realizing Boundary Conditions with Discrete Sine and Cosine\nTransforms,” J. Theor. Comput. Acoust., vol. 29,\nno. 04, 2021, Art. no. 2050021.\n[7] T. Risse, T. Hélie, and S. Bilbao, “Powerbalanced drift regulation for scalar auxiliary\nvariable methods: Application to real-time simulation of nonlinear string vibrations,” in Proc.\n\n[13]\n\n[14]\n\n[15]\n\n[16]\n\n28th Int. Conf. Digital Audio Effects, Ancona,\nItaly, 2025, pp. 126–133.\nR. M. Kirby and Z. Yosibash, “Solution of vonKármán dynamic non-linear plate equations using a pseudo-spectral method,” Comput. Methods Appl. Mech. Engrg., vol. 193, no. 6, pp. 575–\n599, 2004.\nR. Russo, C. J. Webb, M. Ducceschi, and S.\nBilbao, “Convergence analysis and relaxation\ntechniques for modal scalar auxiliary variable\nmethods applied to nonlinear transverse string\nvibration,” Proc. Meet. Acoust., vol. 56, no. 1,\n2025, Art. no. 035007.\nS. Bilbao, C. Webb, Z. Wang, and M. Ducceschi,\n“Real-Time Gong Synthesis,” in Proc. 26th Int.\nConf. Digital Audio Effects, Copenhagen, Denmark, 2023, pp. 21–28.\nA. Torin, “Percussion Instrument Modelling In\n3D: Sound Synthesis Through Time Domain\nNumerical Simulation,” Ph.D. dissertation, University of Edinburgh, 2015.\nD. Schaeffer and M. Golubitsky, “Boundary conditions and mode jumping in the buckling of\na rectangular plate,” Commun. Math. Phys.,\nvol. 69, pp. 209–236, 1979.\nA. D. Muradova, “The spectral method and\nnumerical continuation algorithm for the von\nKármán problem with postbuckling behaviour of\nsolutions,” Adv. Comput. Math, vol. 29, pp. 179–\n206, 2008.\nO. Thomas and S. Bilbao, “Geometrically nonlinear flexural vibrations of plates: In-plane\nboundary conditions and some symmetry properties,” J. Sound Vib, vol. 315, no. 3, pp. 569–\n590, 2008.\nV. Britanak, P. C. Yip, and K. R. Rao, Discrete Cosine and Sine Transforms. Oxford, UK:\nElsevier Ltd., 2006.\nS. Bilbao, M. Ducceschi, and F. Zama, “Explicit\nexactly energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems,” J. Comput. Phys., vol. 472, 2023,\nArt. no. 111697.\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8ac529ab-f84f-4388-b99b-0c7d2f796782", "uuid": "30d07b3c-2e42-4fb7-b162-209cf47784d5", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T07:08:05.050Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "EXPLICIT AND STABLE PSEUDOSPECTRAL TIME-DOMAIN\nMETHOD FOR THE FÖPPL–VON KÁRMÁN EQUATIONS\nVictor Zheleznov1 , Stefan Bilbao2\n2\n\n1\nAcoustics and Audio Group, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK\nSTMS (UMR9912), IRCAM, CNRS, Sorbonne Université, Paris, France\n\nThis contribution has been accepted based on the submitted abstract. The submitted manuscript was not reviewed and is reproduced\nhere without edits or corrections by the conference board. Neither the EAA nor the AAA take responsibility for its contents.\n\narXiv:2608.06139v1 [cs.SD] 6 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nModal synthesis is a widely-used technique for simulation of musical instrument dynamics. In the linear\ncase, a modal decomposition leads to an uncoupled system of damped and forced harmonic oscillators which\ncan be efficiently solved by standard time-stepping\nmethods. However, extensions to nonlinear problems\nare challenging due to the presence of products of\nmodal expansions in the governing equations. In the\ncase of the Föppl–von Kármán plate, the nonlinear\ncoupling between the modes is described by a fourthorder tensor and is prohibitively expensive to evaluate\nin the modal domain. In this work, we propose a\npseudospectral method in which the products are evaluated on a grid in the spatial domain while spatial\nderivatives are computed exactly in the modal domain.\nDiscrete sine and cosine transforms between the modal\nand spatial domains are used to impose simply supported boundary conditions for the plate. Finally, we\nprove non-negativity of the nonlinear potential energy\nof the system and employ a scalar auxiliary variable\ntechnique for explicit and stable time integration in\nthe modal domain. As a result, we reduce the computational cost of modal synthesis while preserving its\nadvantages like a precise control over the simulated\nfrequency range. Sound examples are presented.\nKeywords: nonlinear plate vibration, pseudospectral\nmethod, modal synthesis.\n\n1. Introduction\nHigh-amplitude vibration of a thin plate is perhaps\nthe most dramatic example of nonlinear behaviour\nin musical acoustics, leading to characteristic pitch\nglides and crashes in percussion instruments. The\nFöppl–von Kármán equations are commonly used to\nCorresponding author: v.zheleznov@ed.ac.uk.\nCopyright: ©2026 Victor Zheleznov et al. This is an openaccess article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium,\nprovided the original author and source are credited.\n\nreproduce these perceptually-important effects in audio rate simulations [1]. One of the techniques to\nnumerically solve such equations is known as modal\nsynthesis [2]. This approach falls into a category of\nGalerkin spectral methods where the governing equations are made orthogonal to basis functions of a finite\nseries that approximate the solution [3]. In the case\nof the Föppl–von Kármán equations, this leads to a\ncomputationally-expensive model [2] which requires a\nGPU for real-time applications [4].\nIn this work, we employ a pseudospectral method\nwhere the governing equations are satisfied for a grid\nof collocation points in the spatial domain [3]. Such\nmethods are well-suited for nonlinear problems since\nthey give rise to vector products rather than functional series that require determination of expansion\ncoefficients [5]. We decompose unknown functions\nusing Fourier sine and cosine series to satisfy simply supported boundary conditions for a rectangular\nplate [6]. For time integration, we rewrite the governing equations in the modal domain to ensure exact\ndiscretisation of the linear part of the problem [1]\nand employ a scalar auxiliary variable technique [7].\nThis results in an explicit and stable numerical solver\nfor the Föppl–von Kármán equations in contrast to\nan implicit and iterative scheme previously used by\nKirby and Yosibash [8]. Compared to a modal approach developed by Russo et al. [9] in the related\ncontext of nonlinear string vibration, spatial derivatives are computed exactly in the modal domain rather\nthan approximated by finite differences in the spatial\ndomain.\nThe paper is organised as follows. The continuous\nmodel of a thin plate is given in Section 2. In Section 3\nmodal equations using the pseudospectral method are\nderived, and then discretised in time using an explicit\nand stable numerical solver in Section 4. Numerical\nresults are presented in Section 5. Sound examples\nare available on the accompanying page1 .\n1 https://victorzheleznov.github.io/fa2026\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\f2. Continuous Model\nThe Föppl–von Kármán equations in a scaled form\nover a unit area region D are given as follows [1]:\n∂t2 u = − κ 2 ∆2 u − 2σ0 ∂t u + 2σ1 ∂t ∆u +\n+ κ 2 L(u, φ) + δ(r − re )fe (t),\n∆2 φ = − L(u, u).\n\n(1a)\n(1b)\n\nHere u = u(r, t) is the transverse displacement and\nφ = φ(r, t) is the Airy function. Both are defined\nfor spatial coordinates r = (x, y) ∈ D ⊂ R2 and time\nt ∈ R+ . Partial derivatives with respect to x, y and\nt are denoted as ∂x , ∂y and ∂t , respectively. The\nLaplacian operator is ∆ = ∂x2 + ∂y2 and the biharmonic\noperator is ∆2 . The nonlinearity is introduced by the\noperator L defined for functions α(r) and β(r) as:\nL(α, β) = ∂x2 α∂y2 β + ∂y2 α∂x2 β − 2∂x ∂y α∂x ∂y β.\n\nwhere famp is the excitation amplitude in sec−2 and\nTe is the excitation duration in sec.\nWe can obtain an energy balance for (1a) by taking\nan L2 (D) inner product with ∂t u:\ndH\n= Pe − Pd + Bd + Blin + Bnl + B′nl .\ndt\n\n(2)\n\nwhere ∥·∥D is the L2 (D) norm.\nThe terms Pe and Pd describe input and dissipated\npower, respectively, and are of the following form:\nPe = ∂t u(re , t)fe (t),\n2\n\n[∂n ∂τ u(φ∂τ ∂t u − ∂t u∂τ φ) +\n∂D\n\n(3d)\n\n+ (∆u − ∂n2 u)(∂t u∂n φ − φ∂n ∂t u)] ds,\nT\nwhere ∂n ≜ ∇T\nr n and ∂τ ≜ ∇r τ are normal and\ntangential derivatives, respectively.\n√\nWe consider a rectangular region D = [0, η]×[0, 1/√η]\nwith an aspect ratio η > 0 and employ the simply\nsupported boundary conditions [12]:\n\nu = ∆u = 0,\n\n∂n φ = ∂n ∆φ = 0,\n\nr ∈ ∂D. (4)\n\nAs can be seen, the boundary terms (3) vanish under\nthe boundary conditions (4). Note that the energy H\ntakes the form (2) only under a fixed termination for\nthe displacement u [1]. Initial conditions are assumed\nto be zero, unless stated otherwise.\n\n3.1. Modal Decomposition\nThe boundary conditions (4) allow a closed-form solution for modal shapes [13]. We decompose the displacement u and the Airy function φ using orthonormal\nsine and cosine series with modal coefficients qij (t)\nand ξij (t), respectively:\nu=\n\nMx My\nX\nX\n\n2 sin(kx,i x) sin(ky,j y)qij (t),\n\n(5a)\n\n2αi αj cos(kx,i x) cos(ky,j y)ξij (t),\n\n(5b)\n\ni=1 j=1\n\nφ=\n\nMx My\nX\nX\ni=0 j=0\n\nwhere Mx and My are the number of modes for each\nspatial direction. A scaling factor αi is defined as\n√\nα0 = 1/ 2, αi = 1, i ̸= 0. Spatial components kx,i and\nky,j of a wavevector and a wavenumber kij are:\n\nThe energy H is defined as:\n1\nκ2\nκ2\n2\n2\n2\n∥∂t u∥D +\n∥∆u∥D +\n∥∆φ∥D ,\n2\n2\n4\n\nI\n\n3. Pseudospectral Method\n\nSystem (1) is characterised by a stiffness parameter\nκ and frequency-dependent loss parameters σ0 ≥ 0\nand σ1 ≥ 0, all with dimensions of sec−1 . It is excited\nat the location re = (xe , ye ) by the function fe (t)\ncorresponding to a strike [10]:\n(\n\u0002\n\u0001\u0003\n1\nfamp 1 − cos 2πt\n,\nt ∈ [0, Te ]\n2\nT\ne\nfe (t) =\n0,\notherwise\n\nH=\n\nB′nl = κ 2\n\n2\n\nPd = 2σ0 ∥∂t u∥D + 2σ1 ∥∂t ∇r u∥D ,\nwhere ∇r is the gradient operator with respect to r.\nFor a point on the boundary ∂D with normal and\ntangent unit vectors n and τ , respectively, we can\nwrite the boundary terms as [11]:\nI\nBd = 2σ1\n∂t u∂n ∂t u ds,\n(3a)\nI ∂D\nBlin = κ 2\n(∆u∂n ∂t u − ∂t u∂n ∆u) ds,\n(3b)\n∂D\nI\nκ2\nBnl =\n(∆∂t φ∂n φ − φ∂n ∆∂t φ) ds,\n(3c)\n2 ∂D\n\niπ\nkx,i = √ ,\nη\n\n√\nky,j = jπ η,\n\nkij =\n\nq\n\n2 + k2 .\nkx,i\ny,j\n\nCompared to the displacement u, the Airy function\nφ includes one-dimensional modes when either i = 0\nor j = 0 and a constant mode when both i = 0 and\nj = 0. Without loss of generality, we zero out the\nconstant mode ξ00 ≜ 0 since the Airy function φ is\ndefined up to an arbitrary bilinear function of spatial\ncoordinates [14].\n3.2. Spatial Discretisation\nWe consider a staggered spatial grid defined as:\nxi =\n\n√ i + 12\nη\n,\nNx\n\n1 j + 12\nyj = √\n,\nη Ny\n\ni=0,...,Nx −1\nj=0,...,Ny −1,\n\n(6)\n\nwhere Nx and Ny are the number of collocation points\nfor each spatial direction. We define grid functions\nuij (t) and φij (t) that represent decompositions (5)\nevaluated on the grid (6). We can rewrite them in a\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\fvectorised form as:\np\nu = Nx Ny (Sx ⊗ Sy )T q,\np\nφ = Nx Ny (Cx ⊗ Cy )T ξ.\n\nf2 (q, q) = p\n\nHere vectors u ∈ RNx Ny , φ ∈ RNx Ny , q ∈ RMx My and\nξ ∈ R(Mx +1)(My +1) are formed through row-major\nvectorisation of corresponding grid functions uij (t)\nand φij (t) and modal coefficients qij (t) and ξij (t).\nMatrices Sa and Ca for a ∈ {x, y} are truncated\nmatrices of the orthonormal type-II discrete sine and\ncosine transforms, respectively, defined as [15]:\nr\n\u0014\n\u0015\ni(j + 12 )π\n2\ni=1,...,Ma\n[Sa ]ij =\nsin\n,\nj=0,...,Na −1,\nNa\nNa\nr\n\u0014\n\u0015\ni(j + 12 )π\n2\ni=0,...,Ma\n[Ca ]ij =\nαi cos\n, j=0,...,N\n.\na −1\nNa\nNa\nAssuming Na > Ma , rows of these matrices are orT\nthonormal vectors so that Sa ST\na = IMa and Ca Ca =\nIMa +1 where IN denotes an N × N identity matrix.\nThe Kronecker product ⊗ is used to construct twodimensional transforms.\n3.3. Modal Equations\nWe substitute decompositions (5) into system (1) and\nrequire that it is satisfied on the grid (6). Leftmultiplying\n(1a) and (1b) p\nafter discretisation by\np\n(1/ Nx Ny )(Sx ⊗ Sy ) and (1/ Nx Ny )(Cx ⊗ Cy ), respectively, we obtain:\nq̈ + 2σ ⊙ q̇ + ω 2 ⊙ q = κ 2 f1 (q, ξ) + je fe (t),\n4\n\nk ⊙ ξ = −f2 (q, q).\n\n(7a)\n(7b)\n\n(Mx +1)(My +1)\n\nHere a vector k ∈ R\nis a row-major vectorisation of wavenumbers kij , i = 0, . . . , Mx , j =\n0, . . . , My . In addition, we define k̂ ∈ RMx My by\nexcluding indices i = 0 and j = 0 before vectorisation. Modal frequencies ω ∈ RMx My and damping\ncoefficients σ ∈ RMx My are obtained as:\nω = κ k̂2 ,\n\nσ = σ0 + σ1 k̂2 .\n\nNote that damping coefficients σ can be generalised\nto represent an arbitrary loss profile. The Hadamard\n(elementwise) product ⊙ is used to multiply vectors\nand the power operator indicates elementwise raising.\nThe function fe (t) in (7a) is weighted by modal shapes\n2 sin(kx,i xe ) sin(ky,j ye ) at the location re , consolidated\ninto a vector je ∈ RMx My . This vector represents\ncoefficients of an orthonormal sine series for the delta\nfunction δ(r − re ).\n3.4. Nonlinearity\nSystem (7) is in the same form as in a modal approach [2]. The difference lies in the definition of the\nnonlinear functions f1 (q, ξ) and f2 (q, q):\nf1 (q, ξ) = p\n\n1\n(Sx ⊗ Sy )l1 (u, φ),\nNx Ny\n\n(8a)\n\n1\n(Cx ⊗ Cy )l2 (u, u),\nNx Ny\n\n(8b)\n\nwhere discretised operators l1 (u, φ) and l2 (u, u) give\nexact values of the operator L on the grid (6). To\ndefine these discretised operators, we require differentiation matrices for grid functions u and φ that exactly\nevaluate derivatives of the displacement u and the Airy\nfunction φ on the grid (6), given decompositions (5).\nFirst, we obtain differentiation matrices Dxx,s and\nDxx,c for derivatives ∂x2 u and ∂x2 φ, respectively:\n\u0001\n2\nDxx,s = − ST\nx K̂x Sx ⊗ INy ,\n\u0001\n2\nDxx,c = − CT\nx Kx Cx ⊗ INy ,\nwhere Kx = diag(kx,0 , . . . , kx,Mx ) and K̂x is the matrix Kx without the wavenumber kx,0 . Note that we\ncan split Dxx,s = Dx,ĉ Dx,s into first-order differentiation matrices defined as:\n\u0001\nDx,s = ĈT\nDx,ĉ = −DT\nx K̂x Sx ⊗ INy ,\nx,s ,\nwhere Ĉx is the matrix Cx without the first row. This\nis not be possible for Dxx,c since it accounts for onedimensional modes and a constant mode which are\nnot present in a discrete sine transform. By analogy,\nwe can construct differentiation matrices Dyy,s , Dyy,c ,\nDy,s and Dy,ĉ in the y-direction.\nSecond, we obtain differentiation matrices Dxy,s and\nDxy,ĉ for derivatives ∂x ∂y u and ∂x ∂y φ, respectively:\nDxy,s = Dx,s Dy,s ,\n\nDxy,ĉ = DT\nxy,s ,\n\nwhere Dxy,ĉ φ is exact since one-dimensional modes\nand a constant mode do not affect the value of ∂x ∂y φ.\nFinally, we can define l1 (u, φ) and l2 (u, u) as follows:\nl1 (u, φ) = Dxx,s u ⊙ Dyy,c φ + Dyy,s u ⊙ Dxx,c φ −\n− 2Dxy,s u ⊙ Dxy,ĉ φ,\nl2 (u, u) = Dxx,s u ⊙ Dyy,s u + Dyy,s u ⊙ Dxx,s u −\n− 2Dxy,s u ⊙ Dxy,s u.\nTo mitigate aliasing errors due to products in the\nspatial domain, we employ the 3/2 rule [3] by setting\nNa = ⌈3Ma/2⌉ + 1, a ∈ {x, y}. Under this choice\naliasing would occur only for the highest third of\nwavenumbers which are filtered out by discrete sine\nand cosine transforms in (8). For the next section, it\nis useful to define “brick-wall” filters in the spatial\ndomain as symmetric matrices Hs ≜ (Sx ⊗ Sy )T (Sx ⊗\nSy ) and Hc ≜ (Cx ⊗ Cy )T (Cx ⊗ Cy ).\nThe computational cost of nonlinearity is mainly determined by discrete sine and cosine transforms. For\nsimplicity, assume a square plate so that number of\nmodes and collocation points is the same in both spatial directions. Applying transforms independently\nin each dimension using naive matrix multiplication,\nit takes O(Nx3 ) operations to evaluate f1 (q, ξ). This\nestimate can be reduced to O(Nx2 log2 Nx ) if fast trans-\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\fforms are employed [15]. In contrast, it can be shown\nthat a modal approach [2] would require evaluation of\nO(Mx4 ) = O(Nx4 ) coupling terms with products qij ξlm .\nThis indicates that the pseudospectral method is significantly more efficient for a large number of modes.\n3.5. Nonlinear Potential Energy\nIn view of using a scalar auxiliary variable technique,\nwe require a non-negative potential V (q) so that:\nf1 (q, ξ) = −∇q V (q).\nTo derive this potential, we need to specify product\nrules for differentiation matrices.\nAssume grid functions α and β that are bandlimited\nwith respect to a transform (Sx ⊗ Sy ) and a grid function γ that is bandlimited with respect to a transform\n(Ĉx ⊗ Ĉy ). Considering product-to-sum identities for\nsine and cosine functions, the following product rules\nhold:\nDxx,c Hc (α ⊙ β) = Hc (Dxx,s α ⊙ β + α ⊙ Dxx,s β +\n+ 2Dx,s α ⊙ Dx,s β),\nDxy,s Hs (α ⊙ γ) = Hc (Dxy,s α ⊙ γ + α ⊙ Dxy,ĉ γ +\n+ Dx,s α ⊙ Dy,ĉ γ + Dy,s α ⊙ Dx,ĉ γ).\nBy analogy, we can define a product rule for Dyy,c .\nTaking a dot product of f1 (q, ξ) with q̇, exploiting the\nfact that “brick-wall” filters do not change bandlimited\ngrid functions and using the product rules, we obtain:\nq̇T f1 (q, ξ) =\n\n1\nu̇T l1 (u, φ) =\nNx Ny\n\n1\n(Hc Dyy,c φ)T (Dxx,s u ⊙ u̇) +\nNx Ny\n1\n+\n(Hc Dxx,c φ)T (Dyy,s u ⊙ u̇) −\nNx Ny\n2\n−\n(Hs Dxy,ĉ φ)T (Dxy,s u ⊙ u̇) =\nNx Ny\n1\n=\nφT Hc l2 (u, u̇) = ξ T f2 (q, q̇),\nNx Ny\n\n=\n\nIt can be shown that f2 (q, q) has a zero constant mode\nso that (7b) is fully satisfied.\n\n4. Numerical Solver\n4.1. Quadratisation\np\nWe introduce an auxiliary variable ψ ≜ 2V (q) + ε\nfor an arbitrary constant ε > 0 and rewrite (7a) as:\nq̈ + 2σ ⊙ q̇ + ω 2 ⊙ q = −κ 2 ψg + je fe (t),\nT\n\nψ̇ = g q̇,\n\n(9a)\n(9b)\n\nwhere g ≜ gstd (q) = ∇q ψ. Furthermore, we add a\ncontrol term to (9) as described by Risse et al. [7]\nto reduce\nthe drift between numerical values of ψ\np\nand 2V (q) + ε in discrete time. This results in\nmodification of the coupling term g:\ng ≜ gstd (q) + gmod (q, q̇, ψ),\n\u0010\n\u0011 sign(q̇)\np\n,\ngmod (q, q̇, ψ) = −λ0 ψ − 2V (q) + ε\n∥q̇∥1\nwhere λ0 ≥ 0 is the control parameter in sec−1 .\n4.2. Time Discretisation\nWe choose a time step T in sec, yelding a sampling\nrate fs = 1/T . Then, we approximate q(t) and ψ(t)\n1\nby time series qn and ψ n− /2 on interleaved temporal\nn\nn−1/2\ngrids t = nT and t\n= (n − 1/2)T for n ∈ N. We\nintroduce one-step difference and averaging operators\nδt± and µt± , acting on time series as:\nδt± qn = ±\n\nqn±1 − qn\n,\nT\n\nµt± qn =\n\nqn±1 + qn\n.\n2\n\nUsing centred approximations δt· = δt+ µt− and\nδtt = δt+ δt− for first-order and second-order time\nderivatives, respectively, we define the scheme as:\nδtt qn + 2σ̃ ⊙ δt· qn + ω̃ 2 ⊙ qn =\n1\n= −κ 2 µt+ ψ n− /2 gn + je fen ,\n1\n\nδt+ ψ n− /2 = (gn )T δt· qn ,\n\n(10a)\n(10b)\n1\n\nwhich represents a discretised “triple self-adjointness”\nproperty of the operator L [14]. Using the symmetry\nproperty f2 (q, q̇) = f2 (q̇, q), we obtain:\nξ T f2 (q, q̇) = ξ T\n\n1 d\nf2 (q, q) =\n2 dt\n(7b) T 1 d\n= ξ\n(−k4 ⊙ ξ) = −V̇ (q).\n2 dt\n\nThe potential V (q) is defined as:\nV (q) =\n\n1 2\n2\nk ⊙ ξ(q) 2 ≥ 0,\n4\n\nwhere ∥·∥p for p ≥ 1 is the p-norm. To evaluate V (q),\nwe substitute the solution for ξ = ξ(q) from (7b),\nwhere the biharmonic operator can be easily inverted\nby elementwise division for all but the constant mode.\n\nwhere gn = gstd (qn ) + gmod (µt− qn , δt− qn , ψ n− /2 )\nand fen = fe (tn ). In addition, modal frequencies\nand damping coefficients are adjusted to mitigate\nnumerical dispersion and ensure exact discretisation\nof the linear part of (9a) [1]:\n\u0010 √\n\u0011\n−σij T\n2\n2\n−2σij T\n1\n−\n2e\ncos\nT\nω\n−\nσ\nij\nij + e\n2\n2\nω̃ij = 2\n,\nT\n1 + e−2σij T\n1 1 − e−2σij T\nσ̃ij =\n.\nT 1 + e−2σij T\nWe can obtain an energy balance for (10a) by taking a dot product with δt· qn and employing product\nidentities for discrete-time operators [1]:\n1\n\nδt+ H n− /2 = (δt· qn )T je fen − (δt· qn )T (2σ̃ ⊙ δt· qn ),\n|\n{z\n} |\n{z\n}\n≜Pen\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n≜Pdn\n\n\f×10−13\n\n×10−15\n1\n\n10−1\n0\n\n0\n\n−1\n\n−1\n\nDrift\n\nEnergy error\n\n1\n\nλ0 = 103\n\nλ0 = 0\n\n101\n\n10−3\n10−5\n\n0\n\n50\nTime [ms]\n\n100\n\n0\n\n50\nTime [ms]\n\n100\n\nFigure 1: Relative energy error E n (left) and its enlarged section that reveals numerical quantisation (right).\nHorizontal lines indicate multiples of machine accuracy\n2−52 ≈ 2.2 × 10−16 .\n1\n\nwhere the energy H n− /2 is defined as:\n1\n\nH n− /2 =\n\n1\n1\n2\n∥δt− qn ∥2 + (qn )T (ω̃ 2 ⊙ qn−1 ) +\n2\n2\nκ 2 n−1/2 \u00012\n+\nψ\n.\n2\nn−1/2\n\nThe second term in H\ncan be bounded to guarantee non-negativity of numerical energy [16], resulting\nin a stability condition ωij < πfs , i.e, frequencies\nup to a Nyquist limit can be reproduced. Given the\ndefinition of ωij , the number of modes in simulation\nwould then be smaller than the product Mx My .\nTo initialise the scheme, we require values q0\n1/2\n1\nand\n=\np q and set the auxiliary variable as ψ\n1\n2V (µt− q ) + ε. For time stepping, (10) can be written in an explicit update form after a matrix inversion\nusing the Sherman-Morrison formula [9].\n\n5. Numerical Results\nThe source code used for numerical tests can be found\nin the accompanying repository2 . All simulations\nwere done at fs = 44.1 kHz. A rectangular plate\nwas used with a ratio η = 1.1. Frequency-dependent\nloss parameters were set as σ0 = 1.3 and σ1 = 10−4 ,\nresulting in a T60 decay time of around 10 sec for\nlower frequencies. The simulated frequency range was\nreduced to 17 kHz away from the Nyquist limit to\navoid anomalous behaviour of the scheme [1].\nAs a first case study, a large plate with κ = 8 was used,\nresulting in 1027 modes. To check energy conservation,\nthe plate was initialised in its first mode of vibration\nas q00 = 5 without additional excitation, i.e., Pen ≡ 0.\nStep-by-step relative energy error E n is defined as:\nn+1/2\n\nEn =\n\nHtot\n\nn−1/2\n\n− Htot\n\nn−1/2\n⌊Htot ⌋2\n\nn−1/2\n\n, Htot\n\n1\n\n= H n− /2 +\n\nn−1\nX\n\nT Pdi ,\n\ni=1\n\nwhere ⌊·⌋2 denotes the nearest power of two rounded\ntowards zero. As demonstrated on Fig. 1, the energy\nerror remains constant to machine accuracy during\n2 https://github.com/victorzheleznov/fa2026\n\n10−7\n\n0\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n5\nTime [sec]\n\n6\n\n7\n\n8\n\n9\n\nFigure 2: Relative drift Dn− /2 . Vertical dashed lines\nindicate the moments in time when the plate is excited.\n1\n\nthe simulation.\nTo test drift regulation, the plate was repeatedly excited using the function fe (t) with famp = 2 × 106\nand Te = 2 ms at the location re = (0.17, 0.42). The\n1\nrelative drift Dn− /2 is defined as:\np\n1\nψ n− /2 − 2V (µt− qn ) + ε\nn−1/2\np\n,\nD\n=\nmax 2V (µt− qn ) + ε\nn\n\nwhere the maximum value is taken over the whole\nduration of simulation. Comparison between the cases\nwith and without drift regulation is given in Fig. 2.\nAs can be seen, the maximum value of the relative\ndrift is reduced by two orders of magnitude and the\ndrift steadily decreases towards zero by the control\nterm with λ0 = 103 up to the next excitation. In\ncontrast, the auxiliary variable remains incorrect over\nthe whole duration of simulation in the case λ0 = 0.\nAs a second case study, a small plate with κ = 60\nwas considered, resulting in 127 modes. Fig. 3 provides spectrograms for displacement of the plate at\nincreasing excitation amplitudes, taken at the location ro = (0.64, 0.79). Under a low amplitude, linear\nbehaviour is recovered with distinct modal frequencies. At a higher amplitude, pitch glide effects appear.\nThen, a wideband noise is observed, resulting in a\ncrash-like sound. As demonstrated on the rightmost\nspectrogram in Fig. 3, instantaneous frequencies are\nsignificantly affected by the removal of the control\nterm, leading to audible artefacts. Readers are encouraged to listen to sound examples presented on the\naccompanying page3 .\n\n6. Conclusion\nAn explicit and stable pseudospectral method for simulation of the Föppl–von Kármán equations has been\npresented here. Numerical tests at an audio rate have\nconfirmed energy conservation of the scheme and drift\nregulation properties of the scalar auxiliary variable\ntechnique, which were previously reported only for\nthe case of nonlinear string vibration [7]. Additional\nwork is required to evaluate real-time capability of the\nproposed method in comparison to a finite difference\n3 https://victorzheleznov.github.io/fa2026\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •\n\n\ffamp = 2 × 105 , λ0 = 103\n\nFrequency [kHz]\n\n4\n\nfamp = 2 × 106 , λ0 = 103\n\nfamp = 2 × 107 , λ0 = 103\n\nfamp = 2 × 107 , λ0 = 0\n\n3\n2\n1\n0\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\n0\n\n1\n2\nTime [sec]\n\nFigure 3: Spectrograms for displacement of the plate at increasing excitation amplitudes, as indicated. The third and\nfourth spectrograms are given at identical excitation amplitudes to demonstrate drift regulation of the numerical solver.\n\napproach [10] using an optimised C++ implementation.\nIn addition, extension to other boundary conditions is\nneeded to further evaluate suitability of pseudospectral methods to problems in musical acoustics.\n\n[8]\n\n7. Acknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the SGSAH AHRC\nDoctoral Training Partnership [grant number\nAH/R012717/1]; and the University of Edinburgh.\nThe authors would like to thank Olivier Thomas for\ndiscussions on boundary conditions for the Föppl–von\nKármán equations.\n\n[9]\n\nReferences\n[1] S. Bilbao, Numerical Sound Synthesis: Finite\nDifference Schemes and Simulation in Musical\nAcoustics. Chichester, UK: John Wiley & Sons,\n2009.\n\n[10]\n\n[2] M. Ducceschi and C. Touzé, “Modal approach\nfor nonlinear vibrations of damped impacted\nplates: Application to sound synthesis of gongs\nand cymbals,” J. Sound Vib., vol. 344, pp. 313–\n331, 2015.\n\n[11]\n\n[12]\n\n[3] J. P. Boyd, Chebyshev and Fourier Spectral\nMethods. New York: Dover Publications, 2000.\n[4] R. Diaz and M. Sandler, “Fast Differentiable\nModal Simulation of Non-Linear Strings, Membranes, and Plates,” in Proc. 28th Int. Conf. Digital Audio Effects, Ancona, Italy, 2025, pp. 134–\n141.\n[5] B. Fornberg, A Practical Guide to Pseudospectral Methods. Cambridge University Press, 1996.\n[6] E. S. Wise, J. Jaros, B. T. Cox, and B. E. Treeby,\n“Pseudospectral Time-Domain (PSTD) Methods for the Wave Equation: Realizing Boundary Conditions with Discrete Sine and Cosine\nTransforms,” J. Theor. Comput. Acoust., vol. 29,\nno. 04, 2021, Art. no. 2050021.\n[7] T. Risse, T. Hélie, and S. Bilbao, “Powerbalanced drift regulation for scalar auxiliary\nvariable methods: Application to real-time simulation of nonlinear string vibrations,” in Proc.\n\n[13]\n\n[14]\n\n[15]\n\n[16]\n\n28th Int. Conf. Digital Audio Effects, Ancona,\nItaly, 2025, pp. 126–133.\nR. M. Kirby and Z. Yosibash, “Solution of vonKármán dynamic non-linear plate equations using a pseudo-spectral method,” Comput. Methods Appl. Mech. Engrg., vol. 193, no. 6, pp. 575–\n599, 2004.\nR. Russo, C. J. Webb, M. Ducceschi, and S.\nBilbao, “Convergence analysis and relaxation\ntechniques for modal scalar auxiliary variable\nmethods applied to nonlinear transverse string\nvibration,” Proc. Meet. Acoust., vol. 56, no. 1,\n2025, Art. no. 035007.\nS. Bilbao, C. Webb, Z. Wang, and M. Ducceschi,\n“Real-Time Gong Synthesis,” in Proc. 26th Int.\nConf. Digital Audio Effects, Copenhagen, Denmark, 2023, pp. 21–28.\nA. Torin, “Percussion Instrument Modelling In\n3D: Sound Synthesis Through Time Domain\nNumerical Simulation,” Ph.D. dissertation, University of Edinburgh, 2015.\nD. Schaeffer and M. Golubitsky, “Boundary conditions and mode jumping in the buckling of\na rectangular plate,” Commun. Math. Phys.,\nvol. 69, pp. 209–236, 1979.\nA. D. Muradova, “The spectral method and\nnumerical continuation algorithm for the von\nKármán problem with postbuckling behaviour of\nsolutions,” Adv. Comput. Math, vol. 29, pp. 179–\n206, 2008.\nO. Thomas and S. Bilbao, “Geometrically nonlinear flexural vibrations of plates: In-plane\nboundary conditions and some symmetry properties,” J. Sound Vib, vol. 315, no. 3, pp. 569–\n590, 2008.\nV. Britanak, P. C. Yip, and K. R. Rao, Discrete Cosine and Sine Transforms. Oxford, UK:\nElsevier Ltd., 2006.\nS. Bilbao, M. Ducceschi, and F. Zama, “Explicit\nexactly energy-conserving methods for Hamiltonian systems,” J. Comput. Phys., vol. 472, 2023,\nArt. no. 111697.\n\n12th Convention of the European Acoustics Association\nGraz, Austria • 8th – 12th September 2026 •", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
